Hi gurus,

I must first thank all who replied:
	Eric Gatenby <raptor@mailhub.com>
	Mike Iglesias <iglesias@draco.acs.uci.edu>
	Graham Allan <ALLAN@mnhep1.hep.umn.edu>
	Ian Mortimer <ian@physics.uq.edu.au>

In my original post I reported that ssh experienced runtime problems
(failing to dynamically link libz) and, secondly, I wish to gain other
administrators views and experiences of replacing rsh/rlogin with
ssh/slogin.

The runtime loader problem was resolved by adding two switches to the
configuration script specifying where to find the third party X11 includes
and libraries since GNU's default configuration assumes '/usr/local':
-- cut --
# ./configure --prefix=/usr/apps --x-includes=/usr/apps/include \
        --x-libraries=/usr/apps/lib ... etc etc
-- cut --

In addition, Mike Iglesias pointed out a potential problem with setuid
programs and the environment variable 'LD_LIBRARY_PATH' (from the man
page):
-- cut --
   Security also dictates that the loader will not allow library replacement
   for setuid and setgid programs unless the user is root.
-- cut --

Secondly, I do not want to paraphrase all the extremely detailed advice
and experiences of other more experienced ssh users and administrators. In
preferences I have concatenated below all the replies (contrary to the
rules of this list - sorry Dave Sill). ;-|

(very) Basically, installing and setting up ssh involves the following
steps:

1) use the 'hostinstall' option in the ssh-1.2.25 Makefile which copies
default configuration files and generates a unique key for that
workstation. 

2) run the ssh daemon 'sshd' (see Graham Allan's startup script)

3) maintain the /etc/ssh_known_hosts list with the perl script
make-ssh-known-hosts

I could then merrily ssh around our workstations. However, I found two
problems:
	a) I could not get $HOME/.shosts to work (like .rhosts file)
           so I had to retype my password everytime
	b) the 'make-ssh-known-hosts' script failed so I had to build the
	   /etc/ssh_known_hosts file buy hand. For starters, it always
	   complained about a 'possible typo at line 701'. Plus the manual
           is unclear how to generate the 'hostname list'.

FYI, I have not yet replaced rsh with ssh. But if I can iron out some of
the niggling user and administration problems, ssh looks viable. If anybody
wishes to contribute example 'make-ssh-known-hosts' uses or sshd options I
will repost a update.

For now... that's all folks.

