Did you run ssh-keygen first, to generate a key for the host?  What do
you mean by "to no avail"?  Having your own key will only work (as far
as I can tell) if you either have the same home directory on both systems
or your key in the ~/.ssh/authorized_keys file on both systems.  Using
"ssh -v" will help you figure out why you are having problems.

Also, I think you'll need to have a copy of the public key for the system
(/etc/ssh_host_key.pub) in the /etc/ssh_known_hosts file (alpha 5 needs
alpha6's public key in that file, and vice versa).  You can use the
make-ssh-known-hosts script to do this, assuming you have perl around.
This script depends on you being able to ssh to a host without having to
type a password.

> I don't fancy forcing all our users to generate personal keys. I assume
> there's a way for root to add hosts to a list like the /etc/hosts.equiv
