Subject: [Summary] Program linked with ld core dumps
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078712011780

Thanks guys!  As usual, the list gives instant -- and instantly useful
answers!

The quick and easy answer came from:
	Joshua Rowe [rowe@eksystems.com]
	Anthony Talltree [aad@nwnet.net]
	Steve VanDevender [stevev@hexadecimal.uoregon.edu]
	Allan E Johannesen [aej@WPI.EDU]	
	Ian Mortimer [ian@physics.uq.edu.au]
	Joe Spanicek [joe@resptk.bhp.com.au]
	Phil Farrell [farrell@pangea.Stanford.EDU]

and that was to use the C compiler to do the linking:
	cc -c hello.c
	cc -o hello hello.o

Stephan Zitz [Stephan_Zitz@tds.com] correctly pointed out that it was
probably one of the crt?.o files missing.

David Warren [warren@atmos.washington.edu] gave me the tool to diagnose
it myself, using
	cc -v -o hello hello.c
to see what the C compiler would set up as a link command. That gave the
results:
	ld -o hello -g0 -O1 -call_shared /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/crt0.o
hello.o -lc

Jim Zelenka [jz1j+@andrew.cmu.edu] suggested that I check for a bug in
the code, but that's why I'd recreated the problem with the traditional
hello program; there aren't too many bugs that can hide in four lines of
C:
	#include <stdio.h>
	void main() {
		printf("Hello world!\n");
	}

Again, thanks, everybody!  

