Most systems will continue past Y2K.  The concerns are in two digit year
storage (all systems could think 00 is 1900).  And in how time is
counted.  Unix has used a limited size time counting based on seconds
from the start of time.  When all the bits run out in this counter, time
stops, or restarts.  I think this occurs for most unix (and Mac)
machines in 2023.

I suggest upgrading to 4.0D at your earliest convenience.  Being a
certified platform gives you an advantage when you do have Y2K problems
with the operating system.  It also signifies that Digital will likely
stop all support for earlier releases of it's OS on Midnight Jan 1,
2000.  Go figure...

